From: =?UTF-8?q?Dawid=20Wr=C3=B3bel?= <me@dawidwrobel.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 11:05:42 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 28/41] Documentation: tee: the supplicant's blast radius, and
 what a failed unload strands

Two things a reader needs that the document did not say. Servicing a listener
happens under the QSEECOM call lock, which every QSEECOM caller shares including
in-kernel ones, so a supplicant that stops answering stalls EFI variable access
too -- that is the reason the timeout is short, and it belongs next to the
description of the mechanism rather than being left to be discovered.

And a load whose unload later fails leaves an application that a module unload
makes permanently unreachable, because TZ offers no way to enumerate what is
loaded. Record it as a limitation rather than leaving it as a surprise.

Also explain why ctxdata->closed needs no lock, since it reads like a race.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Assisted-by: Kimi:kimi-k3
---
 Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst | 27 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c    |  6 ++++++
 2 files changed, 33 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst b/Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst
index 865a0d0f3..c21748050 100644
--- a/Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst
+++ b/Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst
@@ -97,6 +97,33 @@ failure. Claiming success without having filled the request buffer leaves the
 application acting on whatever was in it, which has been observed to hang the
 secure world until the watchdog fires.
 
+Only one supplicant may be open at a time; a second gets -EBUSY.
+
+A supplicant that stops answering blocks more than itself
+=========================================================
+
+Servicing a listener request happens with the QSEECOM call lock held, and every
+QSEECOM caller shares that lock -- including in-kernel ones such as
+qcom_qseecom_uefisecapp. So a supplicant that stops answering stalls an EFI
+variable read for as long as the request takes to time out, and an application
+that will not settle can repeat that for several rounds before the driver gives
+up on it.
+
+That is inherent to serialising a single secure-world interface, and it is the
+reason the supplicant timeout is as short as it is. It is worth knowing before
+granting access to the privileged device: the blast radius of a wedged
+supplicant is every QSEECOM user on the system, not just the one that
+registered the listener.
+
+Known limitations
+=================
+
+An application this driver loaded is unloaded when its load session closes. If
+that unload fails, the application stays resident and the driver keeps its
+registry entry, so it can still be reached -- but a module unload at that point
+frees the registry, and nothing afterwards can name or unload it short of a
+reboot. TZ has no enumerate command that would let a later probe recover it.
+
 Security considerations
 =======================
 
diff --git a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c b/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
index c3c2d09bd..50907f3e2 100644
--- a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
+++ b/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
@@ -1223,6 +1223,12 @@ static void qseecom_tee_supp_close_context(struct tee_context *ctx)
 	struct qseecom_tee_listener *listener, *tmp;
 	struct qseecom_tee *qtee;
 
+	/*
+	 * Unsynchronised on purpose. ->close_context() runs from tee_release()
+	 * with no ioctl in flight on this context, so there is nothing to race
+	 * with; the flag exists only because close_context() and release() can
+	 * both be reached and the teardown must not run twice.
+	 */
 	if (!ctxdata || ctxdata->closed)
 		return;
 
